 {:db/id "q-339913/ma-source-field/699f2efcac589426b782437ef3ec23b6d2abadcd59aca6c272a5c5ab3fbce761" :answer-field/id #uuid "93034832-63fb-5330-ae88-5be7fabc5ce3" :answer-field/key "field-5" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-339913/ma-source-field/699f2efcac589426b782437ef3ec23b6d2abadcd59aca6c272a5c5ab3fbce761/0bf1508834bc49a787d08b8b33b37a3b65ce7dcf0b50406376ecc50165a704b6" "q-339913/ma-source-field/699f2efcac589426b782437ef3ec23b6d2abadcd59aca6c272a5c5ab3fbce761/f073cec77042b855fcc95ae59e5c07a0d566930857e27de8effde7dcda1f144f"] :answer-field/correct "q-339913/ma-source-field/699f2efcac589426b782437ef3ec23b6d2abadcd59aca6c272a5c5ab3fbce761/0bf1508834bc49a787d08b8b33b37a3b65ce7dcf0b50406376ecc50165a704b6" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-339913" :question/id #uuid "4f873b9e-7818-5ec8-8091-26b92885da7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-339913" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6x{y}^{′}+10y=4x\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation above, for which the corresponding complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2},$ where $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants. Select the correct options below.$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ \n{{field-1}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe function ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ \n{{field-2}} \na particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$\nThe general solution of the differential equation is given by\n\n$\\,\\,$\n$y(x)=$ \n{{field-3}}\n$+$\n{{field-4}},\n\n$\\,$\nwhere \n{{field-5}} are arbitrary constants." :question/worked-solution "For an inhomogeneous differential equation, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}_{c}(x)$ is the complementary solution (the general solution of the corresponding homogeneous equation), and\n\n- ${y}_{p}(x)$ is a particular solution of the original inhomogeneous equation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check which of the two given functions is a particular solution of our inhomogeneous equation:\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)=x,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =(x)^{′}=1, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(1)^{′}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(0)-6x(1)+10(x)=4x.\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)=x$ $\\text{is}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\n- For ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3},$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}=3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {y}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2})^{′}=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(6x)-6x(3{x}^{2})+10({x}^{3})=4x.\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{p}(x)={x}^{3}$ $\\text{isn't}$ a particular solution of the differential equation.\n\nWe are told that the complementary solution of our equation is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x) \\\\  & =A{x}^{5}+B{x}^{2}+x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A\\text{ and }B$ are arbitrary constants." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-339913/ma-source-field/2e12a8031c21fc9c0a30548dd8367b45e43b43b0e9bd202ac7215eff517889ac" "q-339913/ma-source-field/73556e46bd29c2f6d8c88c7de265b90dd7acd5c3ca33cefb85a7e86ad23e1a0d" "q-339913/ma-source-field/4864dd72507b9d314e9706af0363aa19d6e69291cda0fe5ce9794eed8aa99a66" "q-339913/ma-source-field/8116e759a045418e171bed42f87e3faf96f8a99f8190b0b7d26dca956cf6f543" "q-339913/ma-source-field/699f2efcac589426b782437ef3ec23b6d2abadcd59aca6c272a5c5ab3fbce761"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
