 {:db/id "q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4550c540-2a81-570b-87fb-628f14326571" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2/89cc92a5a256d32ea65399b5196bd1fc92b2d3f166799d13cfa09c67368e2d16" "q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2/de63348529d477cd70740ab9581d04b231678372657a4ecc21dd12e21b5d033c" "q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2/23424e5dffaca1bffc81dd2cc015a958b04177158815d98fdd41149314b3d874" "q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2/ef9beb211f655a6c04dcd4fe42de854d9485336754f46d6dec47ec7e8aed6b34" "q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2/9d34e85dabb5082358af9360d93c7ce717fcea0db3670310b8eab46db4fc35b5" "q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2/34021d3854d8d25832b91720cc624a8e35c2f6fd4d7bab524d021edef59e6673"] :answer-field/correct "q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2/de63348529d477cd70740ab9581d04b231678372657a4ecc21dd12e21b5d033c" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-341136" :question/id #uuid "db14dea0-146d-5df5-a79a-6c50bf00be55" :question/math-academy-id "q-341136" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that the cosine function can be defined in terms of complex exponentials as follows: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)=\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}+{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}{2}}+L{\\frac{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{\\text{i}\\omega t}}=\\frac{1}{s-\\text{i}\\omega },\\,s>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{4\\text{i}t}}+\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-4\\text{i}t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-4\\text{i}}+\\frac{1}{s+4\\text{i}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+4\\text{i})+(s-4\\text{i})}{(s-4\\text{i})(s+4\\text{i})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{2s}{{s}^{2}+16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-341136/ma-source-field/ee42a6f5b6fed2dd4e1cc3d914adc3133517e317fe38d3640fd1f547baa79280" "q-341136/ma-source-field/16e0f403111ba3f56e944af00f48044d53137d60f5deb34b14b97b92dc1579c2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
