 {:db/id "q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3" :answer-field/id #uuid "e8d76374-c302-5673-ab25-1a0eb5568341" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/choices ["q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3/97a23ea6fb635b9f1ee51ed690353d47a20f675209e032e5ed03c67e1d25ac7b" "q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3/de63348529d477cd70740ab9581d04b231678372657a4ecc21dd12e21b5d033c" "q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3/6d8328509dacdbd6081a140366edb8e7c70691a106f66361e38a49ffe1445dee" "q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3/73d2f27a7444698a2a6d45c5f257c48f2948093fe319dc4919ca9d9098731abf" "q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3/9d34e85dabb5082358af9360d93c7ce717fcea0db3670310b8eab46db4fc35b5" "q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3/46af63e5816bfe37d9562c002d5969dd8f5406eadefe6e67411268361eaf7936"] :answer-field/correct "q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3/6d8328509dacdbd6081a140366edb8e7c70691a106f66361e38a49ffe1445dee" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-341140" :question/id #uuid "f89dc302-60ce-5bd9-b567-660590ce563f" :question/math-academy-id "q-341140" :question/problem "Use the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{kt}$ to find the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t).$$\\,L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}=$ {{field-1}},\ndefined for {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, recall that, by definition\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)=\\frac{{e}^{8t}-{e}^{-8t}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity property, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}-{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =L{\\frac{{e}^{8t}}{2}}-L{\\frac{{e}^{-8t}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{8t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-8t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall that \n\n$$\nL{{e}^{kt}}=\\frac{1}{s-k},\\,s>k.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{e}^{8t}} & =\\frac{1}{s-8},\\,s>8 \\\\ L{{e}^{-8t}} & =\\frac{1}{s+8},\\,s>-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)} & =\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{8t}}-\\frac{1}{2}L{{e}^{-8t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s-8}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{s-8}-\\frac{1}{s+8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{(s+8)-(s-8)}{(s-8)(s+8)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{16}{{s}^{2}-64}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{{s}^{2}-64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $L{{e}^{8t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (8,\\infty )$ and $L{{e}^{-8t}}$ is defined for $s\\in (-8,\\infty ),$ we have that $L{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(8t)}$ is defined for\n\n$$\n(8,\\infty )\\cap (-8,\\infty )=(8,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nthat is, $s>8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-341140/ma-source-field/91fbb6d4c2394c753bfa4cffcf19ac3da379634884f86b8f52164798a88ebdc9" "q-341140/ma-source-field/a0fa937a4c48535f455a5dbb0b253157c2a593693e796d1d130ddfe88ad5a7e3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
