 {:db/id "q-346720" :question/id #uuid "fb9ed8ae-2017-567d-8d5e-a0f446be0601" :question/math-academy-id "q-346720" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ passes through $(0,2)$ and is concave down on the interval $(-2,2).$ If ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5,$ estimate the value of $f(0.4)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ and determine whether the approximation is an underestimate or overestimate.$\\,f(0.4)≈$ {{field-1}} and the approximation is an {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(0)+{f}^{′}(0)(x-0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of the function passes through the point $(0,2),$ so we have $f(0)=2.$ Also, we're told that ${f}^{′}(0)=0.5.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+0.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(0.4)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=0.4.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.4) & ≈L(0.4) \\\\  & =2+0.5(0.4) \\\\  & =2.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is concave down on the interval $(-2,2),$ the linear approximation $L(x)$ is above the graph near $x=0,$ and therefore we have an $\\text{overestimate}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-346720/ma-source-field/4c052bfda5dbbc552def5ee2ac251f81a8f2a14ef6e95f7680c27ecbcc78b931" "q-346720/ma-source-field/e17075d6c90fc1fbbdf897a6e96c42a06d2e27210bd39e232a59573724f7f3ea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
